# /etc/default/earlyoom — managed by tasks/setup-memory-safety.sh
#
# Rationale: on unified-memory hardware (DGX Spark GB10) the kernel OOM killer
# often cannot run at all once memory is exhausted — it needs memory itself.
# earlyoom watches from userspace and kills a process *before* that point.
#
#   -m <pct>   act when available MEMORY falls below this percentage
#   -s <pct>   act when available SWAP falls below this percentage
#   -r <sec>   memory report interval (0 disables periodic logging)
#   --prefer   kill these first  (GPU/inference workloads — expendable, restartable)
#   --avoid    kill these last   (losing sshd means losing the box remotely)
#
# MEASURED on this hardware (GB10, driver 580.142): CUDA allocations DO show up
# in /proc/meminfo — a 4 GB cudaMalloc moved MemFree by 4.44 GB. earlyoom
# therefore sees GPU memory pressure and its thresholds are meaningful here.
#
# The same measurement showed cgroup accounting does NOT see cudaMalloc
# (memory.current stayed at 71 MiB for a 4 GB allocation), which is why this
# script relies on earlyoom rather than a systemd MemoryMax= on GPU services.
EARLYOOM_ARGS="-m ${MEMSAFETY_EARLYOOM_MEM_PCT} -s ${MEMSAFETY_EARLYOOM_SWAP_PCT} -r 3600 --prefer '^(python3?|vllm|llama-server|llama-swap|comfyui|triton)$' --avoid '^(sshd|systemd|dockerd|containerd|init)$'"
